# Shell scripts run on Linux. Check them out with LF on every platform.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true gives a Windows working copy CRLF line
# endings, git normalises them back to LF on commit, and the two are only ever
# the same by the time they reach the server. On 2026-09-11 that produced a
# genuinely mixed file: `git checkout -- scripts/monitor-deadman.sh` restored
# 85 CRLF lines, an edit appended LF ones, and shellcheck caught
# "SC1017: Literal carriage return" on a script that `bash -n` had just passed.
#
# It would have shipped correctly, because git normalises on the way in. That
# is exactly the problem: what was tested locally was not what would run, and
# the difference was invisible until a tool that reads bytes said so.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for everything else the containers read verbatim.
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
Caddyfile   text eol=lf

# The vendored subtree is left exactly as upstream ships it. CLAUDE.md forbids
# editing it, and `git add --renormalize .` staged 22 of its files here before
# this line existed — line endings are an edit like any other, and the next
# `git subtree pull` would have reverted them silently.
omniroute/** -text
